            {"title": "1. Preparing Little Hearts", "body_md": "Children learn the Mass long before they understand every word. The greatest lesson is your own reverence: how you dress, how you enter the church, how you kneel and pray.\n\nBefore Mass, help your child settle. A quiet ride, a simple reminder — *\"We are going to God's house to meet Jesus\"* — does more than a lecture. Let them bring a small missal or holy picture rather than toys or a phone.\n\n**Try this:** As you dip your fingers in the holy water and bless yourself at the door, guide their hand too, and whisper the reason: *we remember our baptism.*"},
            {"title": "2. The Parts of the Mass", "body_md": "The Mass has two great tables: the **Table of the Word** (readings and homily) and the **Table of the Eucharist** (the offering and Communion).\n\nHelp your child follow the rhythm:\n\n- **We gather** and make the Sign of the Cross.\n- **We say sorry** (the penitential rite).\n- **We listen** to God's Word — stand for the Gospel.\n- **We offer** bread and wine, which become the Body and Blood of Christ.\n- **We receive** Jesus (or a blessing) and are **sent out** to love.\n\nNaming these simply, week by week, turns a mystery into a familiar friend."},
            {"title": "3. Postures and Their Meaning", "body_md": "Bodies pray too. Teach the *why* behind each posture:\n\n- **We stand** to show respect and readiness — especially for the Gospel.\n- **We sit** to listen, like students of Jesus.\n- **We kneel** to adore, most of all at the Consecration when Jesus becomes present.\n- **We bow** our heads at the holy Name and before Communion.\n\nGentle whispers — *\"Now we kneel, Jesus is here\"* — teach more than a hundred corrections."},
            {"title": "4. The Great Moment: the Consecration", "body_md": "The heart of the Mass is when the priest says Jesus' own words: *\"This is my Body… This is my Blood.\"* The bread and wine truly become Jesus, though our eyes still see bread and wine.\n\nWatch for the bells and the priest raising the Host. Teach your child a tiny prayer to say silently in that moment — the words of St. Thomas the Apostle: **\"My Lord and my God.\"**\n\nThis single habit, learned young, can anchor a lifetime of faith."},
            {"title": "5. Communion and After", "body_md": "Until First Communion, children can go forward for a blessing, arms crossed over the chest, and make a *spiritual communion*: *\"Jesus, I cannot receive You yet, but please come into my heart.\"*\n\nAfter Communion is a precious, quiet time. Kneel together and simply say thank you to Jesus. Teach your child that this is when we talk to the Lord who has come to us — or to whom we long to come.\n\nAs you leave, connect Mass to life: *\"Now let's take Jesus' love home to our family.\"*"},
            {"title": "6. Living the Mass at Home", "body_md": "The Mass does not end at the church door. Carry it into the week:\n\n- Talk about the Gospel at Sunday dinner — *\"What did Jesus say today?\"*\n- Keep a small home altar with a crucifix, a candle and an image of Our Lady.\n- Pray grace before meals and a short night prayer as a family.\n- Point out the liturgical seasons — purple in Advent and Lent, white and gold at Easter and Christmas.\n\nWhen home and church breathe the same faith, children grow up knowing that God is not a Sunday visitor but the Lord of every day."},
